<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Rolfe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1585">1585</a>–<a href="/years/1622">1622</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Rolfe was an English settler in the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> who introduced a sweeter strain of tobacco around 1612, giving the colony a profitable export crop. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a>, daughter of the paramount chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>, in 1614 brought a period of peace between the English and the Powhatan. He died in Virginia in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-rolfe.jpg" alt="John Rolfe" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. W. Glass · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pocahontas_Rolfe_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rolfe's birthplace is unproven, though family documents name Heacham Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>. Historians no longer accept the old belief that his parents were John Rolfe and Dorothy Mason; he had a brother, Henry, whom they are not known to have had. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> then held a virtual monopoly of the tobacco trade, and Rolfe was one of several businessmen who hoped to undercut it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. He illegally obtained seed of a strain grown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, although Spain had decreed death for selling such seed to a non-Spaniard.</p><p>In May 1609 he sailed with his wife Sarah on the Sea Venture, flagship of the Third Supply fleet. A storm drove the ship onto the reefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a>, where their daughter Bermuda was buried. In May 1610 the castaways reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in two ships they had built, and found only 60 settlers alive after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starving-time">Starving Time</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> tobacco, Nicotiana rustica, did not please the English market. In 1611 Rolfe became the first to grow Nicotiana tabacum commercially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, naming his strain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a>, possibly in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a>'s expeditions up the Orinoco River. In 1612 he established Varina Farms on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a>, and the first four barrels of leaf were shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in March 1614.</p><p>On 5 April 1614 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a>, who had been baptised Rebecca by Alexander Whitaker; their son Thomas Rolfe was born in January 1615. In 1616 the Virginia Company brought the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to promote the colony, and Rolfe wrote A True Relation of the State of Virginia. Pocahontas fell gravely ill as they set out to return and was buried at St George's Church, Gravesend, on 21 March 1617.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leaving the sick Thomas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Rolfe went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, which shipped 20,000 pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> tobacco to England in 1617 and 40,000 more in 1618. His letters of 1620 contain the earliest mention of the arrival of enslaved Africans in Virginia, brought by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> man-of-war in August 1619. About 1619 he married Jane Pierce, and their daughter Elizabeth was born in 1620. He may have died in the Indian massacre of 1622, but the evidence is uncertain. His tobacco remained the mainstay of Virginia's plantations for generations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Rolfe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the same years <b>John Rolfe</b>, who had begun growing a sweet tobacco from Caribbean seed, shipped his first crop to England in 1614, and the tobacco boom that followed drove the colonists’ hunger for land along the rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Rolfe's</b> sweet tobacco, first grown around 1612, gave the colony an export crop, and in 1624 the Crown took direct control.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Heacham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Varina Farms, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.91,0.49&amp;year=1609" aria-label="Heacham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Rolfe"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 5605h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 5605h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.91,0.49&amp;year=1609">What was here in 1609? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
